    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-10637",
      "problem": "Given the pentagon $ABCDE$ shown below, find the value of $x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14186732/assets/dc4407245e0639d9055d9518088a46b310f67e897337bd8963e5b1c42a86421f.png)",
      "worked_solution": "A pentagon has $n=5$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D+m∠E & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ {120}^{∘}+{110}^{∘}+2x+{90}^{∘}+x+{40}^{∘} & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ 2x+x+{40}^{∘}+{120}^{∘}+{110}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ 3x+{360}^{∘} & ={540}^{∘} \\\\ 3x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={60}^{∘}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14186732/observations/step-e10637-97e5541564a2c90a357c.html",
